# Commands that run outside the Bash sandbox

Bash commands run in a sandbox. The commands below run outside it, but only
when the Bash call is the command alone. `*` matches any text, and arguments
must come in the order the pattern shows them.

{% for command in claude_sandbox_excluded_commands %}
- `{{ command }}`
{% endfor %}

A call that adds anything to one of these commands stays in the sandbox and
fails, usually with "Operation not permitted". Don't add:

- `cd <directory> &&` in front. The shell already starts in the working
  directory.
- An environment variable or wrapper in front, such as `VAR=value` or
  `timeout 20`.
- A pipe, redirect or other command after it, such as `| tail`, `2>&1`,
  `; command` or `&& command`. Long output is fine, because Claude Code
  truncates it. To read part of it, read the files the command writes.
- A heredoc or `$(...)`. Write a commit message to a file in `$TMPDIR` and
  run `git commit -F <file>`.
- `git -C <directory>`.

When one of these commands fails with "Operation not permitted", check the
call for the forms above and run the command again on its own. Don't ask the
user to change the sandbox settings or to run the command for you until you
have done that.
